Subject: Skew normals likelihood bug?
Message-ID: <TnYwJNAEdqg.kSxblViICQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/TnYwJNAEdqg/m/kSxblViICQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi guys,
I'm trying out some skew-normal distributions, the mean of the likelihood seems to skewing towards the opposite direction of the priors:

library(rstan)
stanmodelcode <- "
data {
  real y;
}
parameters {
  real p;
  real p2;
}
model {
  p ~ skew_normal(1, 10,-1000);
  y ~ skew_normal(p2, 10,-1000);
}
"
dat <- list(y = 1);
fit <- stan(model_code = stanmodelcode,
            data = dat, iter = 66666, chains = 2,
            verbose = TRUE)
library(ggmcmc)
s <- ggs(fit)
ggs_histogram(s)

I wasn't expecting that to happen, should I? If so why?, or is this a bug?

best regards,
