Subject: Re: [stan-users] Sampling a multinormal truncated to lie inside a 2D polygon
Message-ID: <1apPZjmSGT4.6Vla9IKAAAAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <1apPZjmSGT4.eIkePnb1BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <1apPZjmSGT4.eIkePnb1BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/1apPZjmSGT4/m/6Vla9IKAAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Michael,

I see that you did not get a reply (at least on list) so I will write up
my experience with something similar. Recently I was working on a
problem where the parameters in a 2D space were constrained in a
(somewhat) irregular hexagon with two parallel slides which I could put
together from 2 trapezoids, which I then mapped to a rectangle. It was a
bit of algebra, and the Jacobian was not continuous where the two
trapezoids met, but otherwise it was tractable; however, it was also
clear that generalizing it would be extremely tedious.

However, during the holiday break I managed rewrite my parameters in a
way that (1) is easier to handle, (2) makes more sense in the given
context, (3) is easier to interpret and (4) samples faster (higher n_eff
for same number of draws). I don't know your problem domain, but now
instead of thinking of extremely clever transformations my first
approach is to rewrite the problem.

Best,

Tamas

On Wed, Dec 23 2015, Michael Prange wrote:

> I'm trying to write some Stan code to sample a 2D multinormal
> distribution truncated to lie inside a 2D polygon. If x is a 2D sample
> vector, the polygon vertices are the columns of the matrix V, and z is a
> simplex vector whose length equals the number of vertices, then the polygon
> constraint can be described by x = V*z. In other words, x is constrained to
> be an interpolation between the vertices. Here is my Stan code:
>
> data {
>   int nDim;
>   int nVerts;
>   real mu;
>   real sigma;
>   matrix[nDim,nVerts] V;
> }
> parameters {
>   simplex[nVerts] z;
> }
> transformed parameters {
>   vector[nDim] x;
>   x <- V*z;
> }
> model {
>   // mu ~ normal(x, sigma);
>   x ~ normal(mu, sigma);
> }
>
> I provide two versions of the model equation, but both fail for any polygon
> beyond three vertices. When there are three vertices, there is a unique
> linear mapping between x and z, so Stan works well. But for more than three
> vertices, V has a null space, meaning that there are a multiplicity of z
> solutions corresponding to any x. Since z is a simplex, the volume of this
> null space is finite, and varies with the value of x. If I use the volume
> of this null space as the reciprocal of the Jacobian and enter this
> manually using increment_log_prob(), then the sampling is correct.
>
> I'm wondering if there is a better way to sample this problem that avoids
> the expensive manual Jacobian calculation. Any suggestions?

