>> Tamas,
>>
>> Here is the basic idea. Let's say you want to sample x from a multinormal,
>> but want to constrain the samples to lie in a polytope whose vertices, v_i,
>> are the columns of a matrix V. Then the allowed region for x is defined by
>> x = V*nu, where the vector nu is a simplex. Then the naive Stan code for
>> this problem is
>>
>> parameters {
>>  simplex[n_vertices] nu;
>> }
>> transformed parameters {
>>  vector[n_dim] x;
>>  x <- V*nu;
>> }
>> model {
>>  x ~ normal(mu, sigma);
>> }
>>
>> The Stan compiler will inform you that the Jacobian for the transformation
>> from x to nu must be input manually, but for interesting cases the
>> dimension of nu is much larger than the dimension of x, so standard methods
>> can't be used for computing this Jacobian. Since the transformation is
>> linear, the actual values of the derivatives are irrelevant. What is
>> important is the number of nu values that satisfy x=V*nu for a given value
>> of x. I term this multiplicity of nu values the volume of the nu null space
>> for any given x. The Jacobian is then J=1/vol(x). I coded this formula and
>> get the correct sampling (modulo some divide-by-zero issues near the
>> polytope boundary). The main problem is that the computation of this volume
>> is very slow.
>>
>> So while my formulation is correct, I'm wondering if there is a faster way
>> to implement a solution for the general case. I recognize that there are
>> good solutions for particular polytopes, but I am seeking a global
>> formulation. I've seen reflection boundary conditions being used in the
>> literature for Hamiltonian formulations of problems of this sort, but I'm
>> not sure if that is the best approach for Stan either.
>>
>> Michael
