The Stan program attached can't generate the error message included.

Can you send back the corresponding Stan program and output?

On Jan 13, 2016, at 10:22 PM, 'Jason Tilley' via Stan users mailing list <stan-...@googlegroups.com> wrote:

> So now I'm trying to increase the complexity of my model. I'd like to add a random effect. The basic model structure might look in very simple form like:
>
> Y ~ X0 + X1 + X2 + (1|random)
>
> with X0 being the full model intercept. Building on my last model, I have come up with this code based on an example here <http://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.06201.pdf>:
>
> diet_code = """
>
>    data {
>
>      int<lower=0> N; //rows of data
>
>      int<lower=0> K; // number of covariates
>
>      int<lower=0> J; // number of groups (random effects)
>
>      matrix[N,K] x; // predictor matrix
>
>      real y[N]; // response
>
>      int<lower=0, upper=J-1> random[N]; //random effect (my data starts at 0)
>
>    }
>
>    parameters {
>
>      vector[K] beta; //intercept and slope parameters
>
>      real<lower=0, upper=10> sigma; // intercept SD
>
>      vector[J] u; // random intercepts
>
>      real<lower=0, upper=10> sigma_u; // random intercepts SD
>
>    }
>
>    model {
>
>      // local variables
>
>      vector[N] mu;
>
>      // priors
>
>      u ~ normal(0,sigma_u);
>
>      beta ~ normal(0,10);
>
>      // data model
>
>      mu[N] <- x[N] * beta + u[random[N]];
>
>      y ~ normal(mu, sigma);
>
>    }
>
>    """
>
> Surprisingly to me, the code actually compiles. But I get an error:
>
> Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:
> Exception thrown at line 24: stan::math::lognormal_log: Location parameter[1] is nan, but must be finite!
> If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,
> but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.
>
> Rejecting initial value:
>   Error evaluating the log probability at the initial value.
>
>
> Initialization between (-2, 2) failed after 100 attempts.
>  Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.
>
> Using lme in R, I know this model has a solution. This seems it could very easily be something to do with my constraints or priors. What is going on with this model? Am I even in the ballpark?
>

> --
